::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> written as a decimal? {
~0.34#This confuses the numerator and denominator with decimal digits. Divide 3 by 4 to convert the fraction.
~3.4#This places the numerator and denominator beside each other instead of dividing. A fraction represents division.
=0.75#Correct. Since 3 divided by 4 equals 0.75, <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = 0.75.
~75#This gives the percent value without the percent symbol. As a decimal, the value is 0.75.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 0.6 written as a percent? {
~6%#This moves the decimal only one place. To write a decimal as a percent, multiply by 100.
=60%#Correct. 0.6 × 100 = 60, so 0.6 = 60%.
~0.6%#This keeps the decimal unchanged. Percent means out of 100, so the value must be multiplied by 100.
~600%#This moves the decimal one place too far. 0.6 is less than 1, so it is 60%, not 600%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 35% written as a decimal? {
=0.35#Correct. Percent means out of 100, so 35% = <sup>35</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = 0.35.
~3.5#This moves the decimal one place instead of two. Divide the percent by 100 to convert to a decimal.
~35#This keeps the percent number unchanged. A percent must be divided by 100 to become a decimal.
~0.035#This moves the decimal three places. For percent to decimal, move two places left.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 25% of 48? {
~24#This treats 25% like 50%. Remember that 25% is one-fourth of the whole.
=12#Correct. 25% = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, and one-fourth of 48 is 12.
~25#This uses the percent number as the answer instead of finding the percent of 48.
~120#This multiplies by 2.5 instead of 0.25. Convert 25% to 0.25 before multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student reads 72 pages in 4 hours. What is the unit rate in pages per hour? {
~76 pages per hour#This adds the numbers instead of dividing. A unit rate compares to 1 hour.
~68 pages per hour#This subtracts the numbers instead of dividing. Use pages divided by hours.
=18 pages per hour#Correct. 72 ÷ 4 = 18, so the student reads 18 pages per hour.
~288 pages per hour#This multiplies the numbers. A unit rate requires division by the number of hours.
}
